<rss version="2.0"><channel><title>The Verge</title><item><title>We haven’t seen the last of the major tech outages</title><link>https://www.theverge.com/tech/1039851/major-tech-outages</link><guid>https://www.theverge.com/?p=1039851</guid><pubDate>Sun, 22 Nov 2026 17:48:02 -0500</pubDate><description>
											

						
&lt;figure&gt;

&lt;img alt="A number of cursors point toward an unhappy face on a laptop" data-caption="" data-portal-copyright="Photo by Amelia Holowaty Krales / The Verge" data-has-syndication-rights="1" src="https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/chorus/uploads/chorus_asset/file/23318433/akrales_220309_4977_0182.jpg?quality=90&amp;#038;strip=all&amp;#038;crop=0,0,100,100" /&gt;
	&lt;figcaption&gt;
		&lt;/figcaption&gt;
&lt;/figure&gt;
&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;Over the last week or so, we have seen a nationwide FAA ground stop, a multi-day Outlook outage that brought entire companies to a standstill, and a banking outage at &lt;a href="https://apnews.com/article/bank-outage-chase-wells-fargo-truist-50fee2693c22f597fa6ca51a97ef2cda"&gt;JPMorgan Chase&lt;/a&gt;. But the consensus is that &lt;a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/21/us/politics/tech-outages-ai-disruptions.html"&gt;there are more to come&lt;/a&gt;. Director of National Intelligence Jay Clayton told &lt;em&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.cnbc.com/2026/11/22/jay-clayton-tech-outages-faa-microsoft-thanksgiving.html"&gt;CNN&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote class="wp-block-quote is-layout-flow wp-block-quote-is-layout-flow"&gt;
&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;"The American people should be prepared for a great deal of investment in upgrading our systems; they should be prepared for investment in cybersecurity, and they should be prepared that any time there is a step change in technology, a step change in both compute and applications, there will be bumps in the road."&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;Microsoft President Brad Smith issued a  …&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.theverge.com/tech/1039851/major-tech-outages"&gt;Read the full story at The Verge.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
						
									</description></item><item><title>Delta flights in and out of Atlanta delayed due to system outage</title><link>https://www.theverge.com/transportation/1039779/delta-flights-delayed-system-outage</link><guid>https://www.theverge.com/?p=1039779</guid><pubDate>Sun, 22 Nov 2026 12:46:44 -0500</pubDate><description>
											

						
&lt;figure&gt;

&lt;img alt="" data-caption="A Delta Air Lines Airbus A220. | Photo: Saul Loeb / AFP via Getty Images" data-portal-copyright="Photo: Saul Loeb / AFP via Getty Images" data-has-syndication-rights="1" src="https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2025/07/gettyimages-2223798482.jpg?quality=90&amp;#038;strip=all&amp;#038;crop=0,0,100,100" /&gt;
	&lt;figcaption&gt;
	A Delta Air Lines Airbus A220. | Photo: Saul Loeb / AFP via Getty Images	&lt;/figcaption&gt;
&lt;/figure&gt;
&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;All Delta flights in and out of Atlanta are facing significant delays on Sunday due to a problem with the system that calculates a plane's weight and balance. Hartsfield-Jackson Atlanta International Airport is Delta's largest hub and one of the busiest airports in the world. While only Delta is impacted at the moment, disruptions at Hartsfield-Jackson could potentially affect other airports and airlines if delays continue to pile up.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;The issues with Delta's systems in Atlanta come just one week after an FAA outage led to a &lt;a href="https://www.theverge.com/transportation/1033898/faa-ground-stop-sean-duffy-faa-copper-wire"&gt;nationwide groundstop&lt;/a&gt; and thousands of canceled flights. According to &lt;em&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/22/us/delta-flight-delays-system-outage.html?smtyp=cur"&gt;The New York Times&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote class="wp-block-quote is-layout-flow wp-block-quote-is-layout-flow"&gt;
&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;The system that went offli …&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.theverge.com/transportation/1039779/delta-flights-delayed-system-outage"&gt;Read the full story at The Verge.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
						
									</description></item><item><title>Binding of Isaac developer removes AI music from Repentance Plus</title><link>https://www.theverge.com/games/1039770/binding-of-isaac-ai-music</link><guid>https://www.theverge.com/?p=1039770</guid><pubDate>Sun, 22 Nov 2026 12:25:20 -0500</pubDate><description>
											

						
&lt;figure&gt;

&lt;img alt="The Binding of Isaac: Repenentance Plus thumbnail." data-caption="" data-portal-copyright="Image:  Nicalis, Inc." data-has-syndication-rights="1" src="https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/11/j7yPmbNCP4I-HD.jpg?quality=90&amp;#038;strip=all&amp;#038;crop=0,0,100,100" /&gt;
	&lt;figcaption&gt;
		&lt;/figcaption&gt;
&lt;/figure&gt;
&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;The &lt;em&gt;Binding of Isaac: Repentance Plus&lt;/em&gt; has spent the last few days getting &lt;a href="https://store.steampowered.com/app/3353470/The_Binding_of_Isaac_Repentance/"&gt;review-bombed on Steam&lt;/a&gt; after it was discovered that a particular segment of the game &lt;a href="https://www.ign.com/articles/the-binding-of-isaac-dev-removes-undisclosed-ai-generated-music-from-repentance-following-creator-response-review-bombing"&gt;used AI music&lt;/a&gt;. Reddit user &lt;a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/bindingofisaac/comments/1y1qqut/reps_gtavi_runs_music_is_100_generated_by_suno_ai/"&gt;ShweetsStuff&lt;/a&gt; discovered the songs used during a &lt;em&gt;Grand Theft Auto&lt;/em&gt;-themed section. They found several audio files with Suno metadata that pointed back to the tracks on Suno's site.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;Creator Edmund McMillen took to X to &lt;a href="https://x.com/edmundmcmillen/status/2124169167880470745?s=20"&gt;apologize&lt;/a&gt; and claimed he "had no idea" the in-game GTA parody used generative AI music. Adding to the drama, Valve requires developers to &lt;a href="https://www.ign.com/articles/valve-announces-changes-to-how-its-handling-ai-content-on-steam"&gt;disclose how and when they use generative AI&lt;/a&gt; in the development process, and Nicalis, Inc., the studio behin …&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.theverge.com/games/1039770/binding-of-isaac-ai-music"&gt;Read the full story at The Verge.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
						
									</description></item><item><title>Some of our favorite Switch 2 games are cheaper through Black Friday</title><link>https://www.theverge.com/gadgets/1037313/nintendo-switch-2-game-black-friday-cyber-monday-deal-sale</link><guid>https://www.theverge.com/?p=1037313</guid><pubDate>Sun, 22 Nov 2026 12:00:00 -0500</pubDate><description>
											

						
&lt;figure&gt;

&lt;img alt="Screenshot from Donkey Kong Bananza featuring Donkey Kong punching rocks with a young Pauline hanging off his shoulder." data-caption="This ain’t gonna buff out." data-portal-copyright="" data-has-syndication-rights="1" src="https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2025/07/NS2_DonkeyKongBananza_SCRN_25.jpg?quality=90&amp;#038;strip=all&amp;#038;crop=0,0,100,100" /&gt;
	&lt;figcaption&gt;
	This ain’t gonna buff out.	&lt;/figcaption&gt;
&lt;/figure&gt;
&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;Black Friday is a great time to expand your game library because it’s a chance to catch deals that aren’t normally happening throughout the year. That’s true for Nintendo Switch 2 games, as well. In addition to a &lt;a href="https://www.theverge.com/e/1038181"&gt;$50 off sale on one of its console bundles&lt;/a&gt;, Nintendo is also knocking $20 off some of the Switch 2’s best titles. Some of the most notable games include &lt;em&gt;Donkey Kong Bananza&lt;/em&gt;, which is $49.99 (usually $69.99) at &lt;a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0F66KLYVH"&gt;Amazon&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://www.target.com/p/donkey-kong-bananza-nintendo-switch-2/-/A-94694203"&gt;Target&lt;/a&gt;, and &lt;a href="https://www.bestbuy.com/product/donkey-kong-bananza-nintendo-switch-2/J7GSL5JFFJ/sku/6414108"&gt;Best Buy&lt;/a&gt;, and &lt;em&gt;Super Mario Party Jamboree Plus Jamboree TV&lt;/em&gt; for $59.99 (usually $79.99) at &lt;a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0F66JNF9W?th=1"&gt;Amazon&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://www.walmart.com/ip/Super-Mario-Party-Jamboree-Nintendo-Switch-2-Edition-Jamboree-TV-U-S-Version/15920303652"&gt;Walmart&lt;/a&gt;, and &lt;a href="https://www.bestbuy.com/product/super-mario-party-jamboree-nintendo-switch-2-edition-jamboree-tv-nintendo-switch-2/J7GSL5JVYY"&gt;Best Buy&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;Amazon, Best Buy, Walmart, and GameStop are offering discounts on several more Switch 2 games, though their respective end times and availability varies. Nintendo shared that some game sales will end on November 29th. Even if deals dry up for physical games on your wishlist, &lt;a href="https://www.nintendo.com/us/whatsnew/nintendo-announces-black-friday-offers-to-help-spread-joy-this-holiday-season/"&gt;digital game deals&lt;/a&gt; are happening through Nintendo’s eShop until 11:59 PM PT on Wednesday, December 2nd, 2026.&lt;/p&gt;





