&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;Sheherazaad started making ripples in the music world a few years ago with her EP &lt;a href="https://sheherazaad.bandcamp.com/album/qasr"&gt;&lt;em&gt;Qasr&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, which was produced by Grammy Award winner &lt;a href="https://aroojaftab.bandcamp.com/album/vulture-prince"&gt;Arooj Aftab&lt;/a&gt;. Now she's back with &lt;a href="https://sheherazaad.bandcamp.com/album/khudgarzi"&gt;&lt;em&gt;Khudgarzi&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, her reverb-drenched folk-noir debut LP that's primarily sung in Urdu.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;The instrumentation on Khudgarzi draws on traditional South Asian music, including Carnatic and Hindustani styles, but also dabbles in Arabic folk traditions, as well as modern Western electronic music. Trip-hop flourishes and Spaghetti Western guitar brush up against tanbur and ghatam. Sheherazaad's stunning voice sits atop dripping percussion and seasick drones singing about the plight of "The Selfi …&lt;/p&gt;
